From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: John Ericson <John.Ericson@Obsidian.Systems>
Date: Sun, 9 Aug 2026 20:17:40 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] init: do not fprintf() to a NULL FILE when /etc is not
 writable

`write_ioctl_syscon()` opens /etc/ioctl.syscon and then uses the result
without checking it:

    fp = fopen(IOCTLSYSCON, "w");
    (void) fprintf(fp, "%x:%x:%x:%x:0", ...);

On a read-only root -- or one not yet remounted read-write -- that fopen()
returns NULL and the fprintf() faults immediately. Because this is init, the
consequence is not a crash anybody can see: the kernel restarts init, init
reaches the same call and faults again, and the machine sits in a silent loop
at a couple of hundred restarts per second. The only external symptom is

    WARNING: init(8) exited on fatal signal 11: restarting automatically

which names neither the file nor the reason.

The file only caches the console's termios settings across boots and init
runs perfectly well without it, so the fix is to report and return. The
`umask(cmask)` is repeated in the early return because the function raises
the mask to 022 before opening and every other exit restores it.

Found by booting a cross-built illumos with an hsfs root, which is read-only;
`boot_init()` calls this whenever /etc/ioctl.syscon is absent or unparsable,
which on such a root is every boot.

Assisted-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 usr/src/cmd/init/init.c | 19 +++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 19 insertions(+)

diff --git a/usr/src/cmd/init/init.c b/usr/src/cmd/init/init.c
index caeb08bda3..a87b427d0b 100644
--- a/usr/src/cmd/init/init.c
+++ b/usr/src/cmd/init/init.c
@@ -2921,6 +2921,25 @@ write_ioctl_syscon()
 	(void) link(SYSTTY, SYSCON);
 	(void) umask(022);
 	fp = fopen(IOCTLSYSCON, "w");
+	if (fp == NULL) {
+		/*
+		 * Not being able to write this is not fatal: it caches the
+		 * console's termios settings across boots, and init works
+		 * without it. Returning matters a great deal, though --
+		 * every call below would otherwise be made on a NULL FILE,
+		 * and `fprintf(NULL, ...)` is an immediate SIGSEGV. For init
+		 * that is not a crash anybody can see, it is a silent restart
+		 * loop: the kernel restarts init, init faults again, forever.
+		 *
+		 * Reachable on any system whose /etc is not writable when
+		 * init runs -- a read-only root, or one not yet remounted
+		 * read-write.
+		 */
+		console(B_TRUE, "Cannot write %s: %s\n", IOCTLSYSCON,
+		    strerror(errno));
+		(void) umask(cmask);
+		return;
+	}
 
 	(void) fprintf(fp, "%x:%x:%x:%x:0", stored_syscon_termios.c_iflag,
 	    stored_syscon_termios.c_oflag, stored_syscon_termios.c_cflag,
